---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Type digits and you get numbers in words. Type words and you get digits back.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, Input: 4213.75. Output: four thousand two hundred thirteen point seven five.
      Negatives come back with negative in front, and the decimal part is read one digit at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href={toolHref}>number to words converter</a> works out which direction you meant from
      what you typed. Paste an amount straight out of a spreadsheet and it offers to strip the
      currency symbol and the commas for you.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Spell Out A Number →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="hyphens">
    <h2>Where does the hyphen go? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Between a ten and a unit, and nowhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      A compound ten is the only place one belongs. Twenty-one, thirty-four and ninety-nine all take
      a hyphen. Hundreds, thousands and millions do not, therefore four thousand two hundred thirteen
      carries none at all. Every major style guide
      agrees on this, but four-thousand looks tidy enough that people write it anyway.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the common mistake this tool quietly fixes. Avoid inserting hyphens by feel and the
      spelled number will match what a contract, a cheque or a proofreader expects.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How does the conversion work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The number gets cut into groups of three digits, and each group is spelled with the same small
      routine.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, 4213 splits into 4 and 213. The first group takes the word thousand after it, the
      second takes nothing. Inside a group, anything under twenty has its own word, the tens are
      joined to their unit with a hyphen, and a leading digit gets hundred after it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading words back is a running total instead. Each value word gets added, a hundred multiplies
      what is in hand, and a scale word such as thousand pushes the running total up and resets it.
      That is why one hundred five and one hundred and five both come back as 105.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="and">
    <h2>One hundred and five, or one hundred five? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Both are correct English. The difference between them is which side of the Atlantic you are on.
    </p>
    <p>
      American English drops the and. British English keeps it, and a British primary school will
      mark its absence wrong. The converter writes the American form, because that is what cheque and
      invoice software expects and what reads without ambiguity in a legal clause.
    </p>
    <p>
      Going the other way it accepts everything. Commas, hyphens, the word and, and minus in place of
      negative all parse. So you can paste a phrase copied out of a British contract and get digits
      back without editing it first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="decimals">
    <h2>Why is 4.75 read digit by digit? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Decimals are read aloud one digit at a time here, because reading the decimal part as a whole
      number breaks down past two places.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four point seventy-five is clear enough. Four point one two five is not, because one hundred
      twenty-five thousandths and twelve point five are both plausible readings of the same phrase.
      Digit by digit stays unambiguous at any length, which is why aviation, finance and dictation
      all settled on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Money is the exception, and it is worth knowing why. A cheque does not write the cents as words
      at all. Instead it uses a fraction over 100, so 4213.75 becomes four thousand two hundred
      thirteen and 75/100.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cheques">
    <h2>How do I write an amount on a cheque? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Spell out numbers for the whole part, capitalise the first letter, then add the cents as a
      fraction. Banks want you to write numbers in words for a reason worth knowing.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, Input: 4213.75. Output on the line: Four thousand two hundred thirteen and 75/100.
      Fill the rest of the line with a horizontal stroke so nothing can be added after it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reason banks want words at all is that digits can be altered and a sentence cannot. A 1 in
      front of a figure changes everything, but nobody edits four thousand into fourteen thousand
      convincingly. When the two lines disagree, the words win.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scale">
    <h2>Short scale vs long scale, and why a billion moved <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A billion means a thousand million here. It has not always meant that.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the short scale, each new word is a thousand times the last: million, billion, trillion,
      quadrillion. Under the long scale, each new word is a million times the last, so a billion was a
      million million. British English used the long scale until the 1970s, and French, German and
      Spanish still do.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unlike a rounding difference, this one is a factor of a thousand. Instead of assuming, check any
      translated financial figure against its digits. This converter uses the short scale throughout
      and stops at 999,999,999,999,999, which is the largest value a browser can hold as an exact
      whole number.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want the same figure as a numeral, the
      <a href={withBase('/tool/number/roman-numeral-converter/')}>Roman numeral converter</a> handles
      that, and the <a href={withBase('/tool/number/binary-converter/')}>binary converter</a> handles
      base two. Nothing you type here is uploaded.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
